"""Content round 3: misconception tags for the Tests A/B/C and chapter-check items that had no tagged distractor.

Before this round every ITSUP/NETOPS objective already had at least one tagged distractor with a mix-up correction
(70/70, measured with tools/misconception_coverage.py), but 99 of the 129 single-answer knowledge-test items and 17
of the 200 chapter-check items had no tag at all, so a wrong answer on them taught the engine nothing about *which*
wrong idea the student holds. Tests A/B/C are graded and taken by every student, so they were done first.

Rules (same as pass 11/13): a distractor is tagged only when the wrong idea it represents is clear from the item,
its key and its explanation; throw-away distractors stay untagged; labels describe the wrong belief and add no new
curriculum facts beyond what the item explanation already states; the corrective text a student sees is assembled
from existing lesson sections (mastery/content.py). New in this round: every misconception carries ``source``, the
primary source (standard, RFC, NIST publication or vendor documentation) that makes the tagged distractors wrong,
or the course lesson where the item tests the course's own procedure. Reused pass-11/13/cert misconception ids are
used where the wrong idea is the same. Every new entry is ``status: 'Pending SME review'``. Written by Grok Bot,
Oct 18 2026.

Finished by the round-3 takeover worker (Oct 18 2026, after the first worker stalled at IT-6.3): every tag below was
checked against the items the engine actually serves (exact option text, not the key, same objective). Three
chapter-check tags were updated to the pass-15 option-length rewrites (Q4.7, Q4.9, Q6.7). Two drafted ids were dropped
in favour of existing ids for the same wrong idea (A1.23 -> MC-C-IT8-REPAIR-TOOL, NQ6.4 -> MC-C-NO6-NO-ROLLBACK).
Left untagged on purpose: Q13.6 and NQ5.5 (their distractors are throw-aways, not a wrong idea the explanation names),
and individual throw-away distractors on otherwise tagged items.
"""

STATUS = 'Pending SME review'


def _m(obj, label, fix, terms, source):
    return {'obj': obj, 'label': label, 'fix': fix, 'terms': terms, 'source': source, 'status': STATUS}


